  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality and equal opportunities and treatment for all in the world of work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Paid care work and unpaid care work, which constitute the structure of the care economy, are essential to all other work. A well-functioning and robust care economy contributes to a healthier present and future workforce, creates jobs, supports businesses, enhances productivity, boosts aggregate demand, and promotes gender equality.
The highest burdens of unpaid care work, and the poor working conditions and pay in subsectors of the care economy such as in domestic work, typically fall upon already vulnerable groups of women, highlighting intersectional and compounding disadvantages for ethnic minority, indigenous, migrant, and rural women.
This situation leads to significant gender gaps in the labor market, including lower labor force participation rates for women, weaker social security coverage, income disparities, and occupational segregation. 
The ILO has recognized these challenges and has provided past technical assistance based on the landmark Resolution on Decent Work in the Care Economy, which emphasizes the need for urgent action to ensure decent work in this sector.
For the current biennium, the ILO&apos;s strategy focuses on knowledge creation and dissemination, developing partnerships, and expanding services through resource mobilization. The ILO aims to consolidate existing knowledge, generate new insights, and engage with researchers and specialists to build a conceptual model on innovative initiatives related to care work. The strategy includes mapping existing projects, developing an analytical framework, and conducting staff training on the care economy. Additionally, the ILO plans to hold technical dialogue series, strategic visibility events, and support the production and dissemination of data on the care economy in the Asia-Pacific region.</narrative>
  </description>
